In this lesson we'll look at three different ways to flatten a 3D object onto a flat piece of paper. Each one keeps some information and throws away other information, so they're useful for different jobs. First up, top, front and side views, sometimes called orthogonal drawings. You imagine looking straight at the object from above, from the front, and from the side, and you draw exactly what you'd see. Each view is a flat 2D shape, so lengths and right angles stay true, but you lose any sense of depth. These are perfect when you need exact measurements. Next, perspective drawings, like isometric drawings. Here the front face stays square, but the sides slant back at an angle, often around 30 degrees, so you can see several faces at once and the object actually looks solid. You get a great 3D impression, but those slanted edges are foreshortened, so you can't measure off them directly. Finally, the net. That's when you mentally unfold the object so every face lies flat, joined along the edges. The net shows every face in its true size, which makes it perfect for calculating surface area or cutting the shape out to fold back into a solid. One heads up, a single view is rarely enough to rebuild the object. Usually you need two views together, like top plus front, to pin down every edge. Now let's see it in action.